From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: sunxi: parse the generic power-source property

sunxi supplies its own dt_node_to_map(), so the generic pinconf parser never
sees these nodes: sunxi_pctrl_build_pin_config() walks the node by hand and
knows exactly two properties, drive-strength and bias. "power-source" is in
pinconf-generic's table (pinconf-generic.c, "power-source" ->
PIN_CONFIG_POWER_SOURCE) and is therefore accepted by the schema and silently
dropped by this driver.

That is not theoretical. On 2026-07-29 an A733 patch pair added a
PIN_CONFIG_POWER_SOURCE handler for the SD bank's I/O voltage and a DT that
carries power-source in the mmc_1v8 pinctrl state. It passed the automated checks
and review, but did nothing on hardware: the handler never ran and the voltage register
kept its reset default. The DT half then made it worse than a no-op, because
advertising sd-uhs-sdr104 without the pads following made the mmc core switch
the card to 1.8 V against 3.3 V pads and the card stopped enumerating.

So parse it, in the same shape as the two properties already there: a
has_*_prop() for the count, a parse_*_prop() that reads the u32, and one more
pinconf_to_config_packed() in the builder. Drivers that do not implement
PIN_CONFIG_POWER_SOURCE are unaffected -- sunxi_pconf_set() returns -ENOTSUPP
for a parameter its register map does not cover, exactly as before, and a node
without the property produces the same config array as today.

The argument stays as written in the DT. pinconf-generic deliberately leaves
its meaning to the driver; on the sunxi controllers that can switch a bank's
I/O voltage it is microvolts, which is what the consumer's regulator would
otherwise supply. That contract belongs in the SoC driver that implements the
parameter, and is documented there.

Placed early in the series, ahead of its SD/MMC consumer: this is the shared
pinctrl core and the capability is generic to sunxi, not A733-specific. The
A733 half -- the register write for bank PF -- stays with the storage patches.

Verified: applies at fuzz 0, builds. NOT verified: any hardware effect. The
observable that decides it is the A733 SD bank switching to 1.8 V through this
path, which needs the SoC handler and the DT that consume it.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/sunxi/pinctrl-sunxi.c
@@ -253,6 +253,26 @@
 		of_property_present(node, "allwinner,drive");
 }
 
+static bool sunxi_pctrl_has_power_source_prop(struct device_node *node)
+{
+	return of_property_present(node, "power-source");
+}
+
+/*
+ * The generic binding leaves the meaning of the argument to the driver; on
+ * sunxi the pin controllers that can switch a bank's I/O voltage take it in
+ * microvolts, matching the regulator the pads would otherwise follow.
+ */
+static int sunxi_pctrl_parse_power_source_prop(struct device_node *node)
+{
+	u32 val;
+
+	if (of_property_read_u32(node, "power-source", &val))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	return val;
+}
+
 static int sunxi_pctrl_parse_bias_prop(struct device_node *node)
 {
 	u32 val;
@@ -359,6 +379,8 @@
 		configlen++;
 	if (sunxi_pctrl_has_bias_prop(node))
 		configlen++;
+	if (sunxi_pctrl_has_power_source_prop(node))
+		configlen++;
 
 	/*
 	 * If we don't have any configuration, bail out
@@ -395,6 +417,17 @@
 		pinconfig[idx++] = pinconf_to_config_packed(pull, arg);
 	}
 
+	if (sunxi_pctrl_has_power_source_prop(node)) {
+		int uV = sunxi_pctrl_parse_power_source_prop(node);
+
+		if (uV < 0) {
+			ret = uV;
+			goto err_free;
+		}
+
+		pinconfig[idx++] = pinconf_to_config_packed(PIN_CONFIG_POWER_SOURCE,
+							   uV);
+	}
 
 	*len = configlen;
 	return pinconfig;
